PWHASH(3)                      P32W Unix                       PWHASH(3)

NAME
       pwhash, pwcheck - hash and check passwords

SYNOPSIS
       #include <pwd.h>

       char *pwhash(const char *password, const char *salt);
       int pwcheck(const char *password, const char *stored);

DESCRIPTION
       pwhash makes the form in which /etc/passwd keeps a password.
       salt is read as a hexadecimal number, of which the low 16
       bits are used (it should be 4 hex digits, chosen at random
       for each new password). The result is the salt, a $, and a
       64-bit hash, all in lower-case hex: 21 characters such as

           3f1a$9c0e5b2d77a01f4e

       The hash runs two 32-bit lanes over the salt and the password
       2000 times, to make guessing slow. It is the same function as
       the host's tools/mkpasswd.py, which makes the first
       /etc/passwd.

       pwcheck tells whether password is the one stored as the
       password field of an entry (pw_passwd, getpwent(3)): an empty
       stored field accepts any password; one starting with *, or of
       any length but 21, accepts none; otherwise it hashes password
       with the salt at the start of stored and compares.

RETURN VALUE
       pwhash returns a static string, overwritten by the next call.
       pwcheck returns 1 if the password matches, else 0.

EXAMPLES
       char salt[8];
       sprintf(salt, "%04x", (rand() ^ getpid() ^ time(0)) & 0xFFFF);
       newfield = pwhash(typed, salt);

       if (!pwcheck(typed, pw->pw_passwd))
           fprintf(stderr, "Login incorrect\n");

SEE ALSO
       login(1), passwd(1), su(1), getpwent(3), passwd(5)

BUGS
       This is not V7's crypt, and the hash is not compatible with
       any other system's. It is meant for a machine on two serial
       lines: anyone who can read /etc/passwd (everyone) can try
       passwords against it offline, and 2000 rounds are quick on a
       modern computer.
